\value{
A \code{tt_table} object. It is returned invisibly when \code{xlsx}, \code{csv}
or \code{markdown} writes a file, so assign it (\code{tab <- regtab(...)}) and
print it to see it. Its \code{stored} element holds Stata's \code{r()} results:
\code{coef_label}, \code{ci_level},
\code{methods} (a sentence built from the models: the estimates by effect
scale, the model by its Stata command, family and link,
"survey-weighted" for \code{svyglm}, and "univariable" for one predictor
variable, where \code{strata()}, \code{cluster()}, \code{frailty()} and \code{offset()}
terms are not predictors and a \code{cmprsk::crr} fit with several
coefficients gets no adjective; Stata builds it from the estimate
header instead, so it differs where Stata's names the wrong model),
\code{table} (display-scale estimates per row and model), \code{N_rows},
\code{N_cols}, \code{N_models}, \code{stars}, per-model statistics (\code{n_1}, \code{aic_1}, ...),
and, when written, \code{xlsx}, \code{sheet}, \code{markdown}, \code{markdown_rows},
\code{markdown_cols}; and two R-only entries: \code{ci_method} (\code{"wald"} or
\code{"profile"}), and, when a model's variance fell back to another source
(mixed models only, with a warning), \code{vce_fallback}, one description
per model (\code{""} for none). See \code{\link[=as_forest_data]{as_forest_data()}} for the estimates as
data.

\verb{$meta$sample_accounting} carries the source ledger described in
\code{\link[=tt_table]{tt_table()}}, with separate fitted populations for each model or
imputation and explicit unavailable original-input counts.
}
\description{
R implementation of the Stata \code{regtab} command. Where Stata reads the active
\code{collect}, this function takes one or more fitted models and renders them
side by side: one block of estimate, confidence-interval, and p-value columns
per model, variable-label header rows for factors, indented levels, and an
italic reference row for each base category.
}
\details{
Pass fitted models (\code{regtab(fit1, fit2)}, or a named list to label
them); the result is a \code{tt_table} that prints, and \code{xlsx}, \code{csv} and
\code{markdown} write it. The effect scale (OR, HR, IRR, Coef.) is detected per
model unless \code{coef} is given, and ratio-scale estimates are
exponentiated. Argument names mirror the Stata options.

Common tasks, and the sections below that cover them:
\itemize{
\item Which models work: "Supported models"; ordinal, multinomial, count,
survival and competing-risks models: "Ordinal, multinomial, count, and
survival models"; \code{lme4}, \code{glmmTMB}, \code{nlme} and \code{geepack} fits: "Mixed
models and GEE"; any other model, as a data frame of its estimates:
"Data-frame input".
\item Choose rows (\code{keep}, \code{drop}): "Rows and keys"; interaction labels:
"Interactions".
\item Add N, events, AIC and other statistics (\code{stats}): "Model statistics".
\item Robust, clustered or user-supplied standard errors, survey and IPTW
weights, marginal structural models (\code{vce}, \code{cluster}): "Standard
errors and intervals" and "Weights, robust variance, IPTW and marginal
structural models", and \code{vignette("weighted-analyses")}.
\item Multiply imputed data: "Multiple imputation".
\item Univariable (crude) models in one column: \code{\link[=regtab_uv]{regtab_uv()}}; whole tables
side by side or one under another: \code{\link[=tt_merge]{tt_merge()}}, \code{\link[=tt_stack]{tt_stack()}}.
}

\code{vignette("regression-tables")} works through these with examples.
}
\section{Supported models}{

\code{lm}, \code{glm} (including \code{MASS::glm.nb}), and \code{survival::coxph};
\code{MASS::polr}, \code{ordinal::clm}, \code{nnet::multinom}, \code{pscl::zeroinfl},
\code{pscl::hurdle}, \code{survival::survreg} (and \code{AER::tobit}), Fine-Gray
\code{coxph} on \code{survival::finegray()} data, \code{cmprsk::crr}, and data frames;
\code{lme4::lmer}, \code{lme4::glmer}, \code{glmmTMB::glmmTMB}, \code{nlme::lme}, and
\code{geepack::geeglm} (see the sections on ordinal, multinomial, count and survival
models, on mixed models and GEE, and on data-frame input);
\code{survey::svyglm} and \code{WeightIt::glm_weightit} (see the
section on weights). The gate is
an allow-list on the fit's first class: any other class, including a
subclass of a supported one (\code{mgcv::gam},
\code{survey::svycoxph}, a \code{coxph} with a \code{frailty()} term), stops with an
error naming it; pass such a model as a data frame. An \code{lmerTest} fit
(class \code{lmerModLmerTest}) is converted with \code{as(fit, "lmerMod")}, with a
note: regtab reports Stata \code{mixed}'s z statistics, not lmerTest's
Satterthwaite t tests. Multiply imputed fits (\code{mice}'s \code{mira}, or
\code{\link[=tt_mi]{tt_mi()}}) are pooled (see the section on multiple imputation); a pooled
\code{mice} result (\code{mipo}) is refused with a hint to pass the \code{mira}. To
show the imputations side by side instead, pass \code{fit$analyses} as a
list. A list passed to \code{regtab()} must hold only fitted models (or data
frames): any other element is an error naming it.

Limitations:
\itemize{
\item Collinear terms: R's \code{lm()}/\code{glm()} alias the later of linearly
dependent columns, while Stata may omit another (\verb{regress y x1 x2 x3}
with \code{x3 = 2 * x2} omits \code{x2} in Stata and \code{x3} in R; \verb{mi estimate}
likewise). The fitted model is the same; the Omitted row and the
coefficient shown on the other row differ. Drop the redundant term
before fitting for Stata's layout.
\item Multi-equation interaction rows follow Stata's colnames for two-way
terms (\verb{1.female#index_age}, \code{treatment#age_z}, with Reference rows)
or, with \code{interactions = "fvgen"}, fvgen's labels (\verb{part1 x part2});
higher-order terms render natively, untested against Stata.
\item \code{pscl::hurdle} has no Stata counterpart (it is shown in \code{churdle}'s
layout): its standard errors are the observed information from the
analytic score, checked against the logit GLM (zero part) and pscl's
own \code{vcov()}, not against Stata.
\item \code{glmer} families with an estimated scale (Gamma, or gaussian with a
non-identity link) keep lme4's \code{vcov()} for the fixed effects and get
no random-effects intervals (with a warning on every call, recorded
in \code{stored$vce_fallback}): lme4 holds their scale outside the deviance
function regtab differentiates. A \code{glmmTMB} fit whose full variance
cannot be formed likewise warns, and its random-effects rows have no
interval.
\item Mixed-effects Cox models (\code{coxme::coxme}) have no Stata equivalent
and are not supported: Stata's \code{mestreg} is a parametric survival
model, and \verb{stcox, shared()} is a gamma frailty model.
}
}

\section{Rows and keys}{

Rows follow the formula as written, the union of all models' rows in
order of first appearance, with the intercept last. Each row has a
Stata-style key used by \code{keep}/\code{drop}: \code{age} (a numeric term), \code{education}
(a factor's header row), \verb{2.education} (a level), \verb{_cons} (intercept),
and \verb{1.foreign#3.rep78} (an interaction cell). A bare name selects the
variable's rows and every interaction containing it; \code{i.}/\code{c.} prefixes
are ignored; R coefficient names (\code{educationSecondary}) also work. A
\verb{##} term (\code{keep = "i.foreign##i.rep78"}) matches nothing and errors,
where Stata keeps only the parent row. Keys are Stata-style in fvgen mode
too (\verb{1.foreign#2.pclass}, \code{keep = "foreign"}), not fvgen's generated
variable names. A factor made in the formula keeps the formula's text as
its name: with \code{factor(cyl)} the keys are \code{factor(cyl)} and
\verb{6.factor(cyl)}, so \code{keep = "cyl"} matches nothing (and errors); convert
the column in the data instead, or write \code{keep = "factor(cyl)"}.

A level's code is the Stata value code when the factor keeps a
\code{"labels"} attribute, as \code{\link[=tt_as_factor]{tt_as_factor()}} does for \code{haven::read_dta()}
data; else the level itself when it is an integer; \code{0}/\code{1} for a logical
(shown as an unlabelled 0/1 variable); a character predictor's sorted
position (Stata's \code{encode}); otherwise the level's position (1, 2, ...).
\code{haven::as_factor()} drops the codes, so its 0/1 \code{foreign} is keyed
\verb{1.foreign}/\verb{2.foreign}: regtab prints a note when native interaction
keys or \code{keep}/\code{drop} level terms rely on such positional codes. Rows
of several models are joined as Stata joins them, by level: Stata by
value code, regtab by the code when there is one and else by the level
itself. So a factor relevelled in one model (each model's Reference on
its own level's row), a subset model that lacks a level (its row empty
there) and a character predictor missing a value in one model line up;
where the models number a variable's levels differently, its keys
follow one numbering over all models (model 1's levels in order, a
level only a later model has placed after its predecessor; a character
predictor's sorted values, as Stata's \code{encode}), and the rows follow
it. A variable whose levels still cannot be matched (a factor mixing
numeric and text levels) is refused, never shown with one model's
estimate under another level's label.
Character predictors take R's level order at fit time, which depends on
the locale's collation; Stata's \code{encode} sorts bytewise (the C locale).

Factors must use treatment-type contrasts (\code{contr.treatment} with any
base, or \code{contr.SAS}, Stata's \verb{ib(last).}), since a Reference row claims a
contrast against that level: ordered factors (polynomial contrasts),
\code{contr.sum}, \code{contr.helmert} and custom contrasts are refused. A term
with several columns (a matrix, \code{poly()}, splines) gets one row per
column, keyed and labelled by its coefficient name.

Variable labels come from the \code{"label"} attribute of the model frame
columns; for a fit with \verb{subset =} they are read from the data at
\code{regtab()} time, with a warning if the data no longer match the fit.
}

\section{Interactions}{

By default (\code{interactions = "fvgen"}) the table matches Stata's \code{fvgen} +
\code{regtab} workflow, with factor main effects of interacted variables as
flat rows labelled by level (optionally \code{vsref = "(vs. @)"}) and one row
per interaction cell labelled \verb{part1 x part2} (parts joined by \code{xsymbol}
with a space on each side); \code{I(x^2)} reads \verb{<label>} with a superscript
two. As in fvgen, the factor comes first whatever the formula's order
(\code{mpg * foreign} reads \verb{Foreign x Mileage}), generated labels are cut to
80 characters, and only two-way terms are supported (\code{f * I(x^2)} is
three-way). \code{interactions = "native"} reproduces native \code{regtab}: a
\code{foreign#rep78} parent row and raw \verb{1.foreign#3.rep78} rows, with base
cells \code{refcat}, empty cells \code{emptylabel}, and aliased cells \code{omitlabel}.
}

\section{Model statistics}{

\code{stats} adds rows below the estimates, in Stata tabtools 2.1.12's fixed
order:
\itemize{
\item \code{n}: Observations, or Subjects when a model has them (survival
models; \verb{\%12.0fc});
\item \code{events}: Events, the number of failures (Stata's e(N_fail)) of a
\code{coxph} (\code{stcox}: weighted by the case weights, as after \code{stset [pweight]}), Fine-Gray (\code{stcrreg}: the failures of interest) or
\code{survreg} fit (\code{streg}: weighted by its case weights); blank for
other models, and for \code{cmprsk::crr}, which keeps no failure count.
A weighted fit's failures are counted on its observations (for a
\code{tt()} model, not the records survival expands it into), from the fit
or from its data re-sorted with their row names reset; when neither
pairs its statuses with its weights (\code{y = FALSE} with data that
cannot be checked) the cell is blank, with a note;
\item \code{groups}: Groups (mixed models);
\item \code{mi_m}: Imputations, the number of imputations of a multiply imputed
model (Stata's e(M_mi));
\item \code{aic}, \code{qic}, \code{bic}, \code{ll}: AIC (QICu for a GEE model), QICu, BIC,
Log-likelihood (\verb{\%12.2f});
\item \code{icc}: ICC (mixed models);
\item \code{r2}: R-squared, the pseudo R-squared where there is no R-squared,
else the adjusted R-squared (\verb{\%5.3f});
\item \code{r2_a}: Adjusted R-squared (\code{lm}, Stata \code{regress}; \verb{\%5.3f});
\item \code{rmse}: Root MSE (\code{lm}), the square root of the (weighted) residual sum
of squares over N - k, the weights rescaled to mean 1 as \code{regress}
rescales \verb{[aweight]}s (\verb{\%9.3f});
\item \code{F}: F statistic (\verb{\%9.2f}), shown only for linear regression (\code{lm},
and a Gaussian identity-link \code{survey::svyglm}, Stata's \code{svy: regress}), never for \code{glm} or other models: the classical F of
\code{regress}, the Wald F with the robust or cluster-robust variance under
\code{vce = "robust"}/\code{"cluster"} (blank, as in Stata, when there are fewer
clusters than coefficients), the design-based adjusted Wald F of \code{svy: regress}; a constant-only model shows 0, Stata's e(F);
\item \code{fmi}: Largest FMI, the largest fraction of missing information over
the coefficients of a multiply imputed model (\verb{\%6.4f});
\item \code{vce} (R only; Stata regtab has no such token): Standard errors, each
model's variance as text, in the words of the \code{vce_note} footnote
("robust", "robust, clustered by id", "user-supplied", "survey design
(linearized)", ...), and "model-based" for the command's model-based
default; under \code{vce = "model"} it names the fit's own variance (a
robust \code{coxph}/\code{survreg} keeps survival's sandwich, a \code{geeglm}
geepack's sandwich or jackknife). Blank for a data frame, whose
standard errors regtab cannot know.
}

The full-precision values are stored as \code{n_1}, \code{events_1}, \code{aic_1},
..., \code{F_1}, \code{fmi_1} (one per model), as Stata's \code{r()} (\code{vce} stores
nothing). \code{stat_fun} adds R-only rows below them.
}

\section{Standard errors and intervals}{

Confidence intervals are Wald intervals by default, on the same
reference distribution as the reported p-value, and match what the
Stata command reports (\code{vce = "stata"}):
\itemize{
\item \code{lm} (Stata \code{regress}): \code{vcov()}, Student t with the residual degrees
of freedom.
\item \code{glm}, every family and link (Stata \code{logit}, \code{probit}, \code{cloglog},
\code{poisson}, \code{glm}): the normal distribution, also for families with an
estimated dispersion (gaussian, Gamma, quasi), because Stata's \code{glm}
reports z statistics. Standard errors come from the observed
information at the final estimates, Stata's default \code{vce(oim)},
multiplied by the Pearson dispersion where it is estimated (Stata's
\code{scale(x2)}). For canonical links (logit, log-Poisson, identity) this is
the Fisher information at the final estimates; R's \code{vcov()} evaluates it
at the previous IRLS iterate, which moves standard errors in the 7th
digit, and for other links (probit, cloglog, Gamma-log) it uses the
expected information, which can move the CI and p cells. The header
follows Stata's family/link rule: odds ratios for binomial logit,
incidence rate ratios for Poisson log, \code{Coef.} otherwise, so a
log-binomial fit (\code{binomial(link = "log")}, Stata's \verb{glm, family(binomial) link(log)} without \code{eform}) shows log risk ratios under \code{Coef.}; its
methods sentence names "log-binomial regression". A \code{glm} with the
\code{MASS::negative.binomial(theta)} family (a known theta, Stata's \verb{glm, family(nbinomial k)} with k = 1/theta) has scale 1, as in Stata, not
the Pearson dispersion \code{summary()} uses; its weights are analytic
weights (\code{glm [aweight]}, which Stata allows for this family).
\code{ci_method = "profile"} is refused for it: R's profile of this family
uses the Pearson dispersion.
\item \code{MASS::glm.nb} (Stata \code{nbreg}): the coefficient block of the inverse
joint observed information over the coefficients and ln(alpha). With
a link other than log (\code{link = identity}, \code{sqrt}) it is Stata's \verb{glm, family(nbinomial ml) link()}: no ln(alpha) row, and the variance of the
coefficients at a fixed alpha. Stata takes that alpha from the log-link
\code{nbreg}, while \code{glm.nb()} estimates theta jointly with the other link's
coefficients, so estimates and standard errors differ from Stata's:
about 1e-5 (relative) unweighted on the probe data, and more with
weights (3\% on a standard error on \code{auto} with \verb{[iw=turn]}, identity
link).
\item \code{survival::coxph} (Stata \code{stcox}): \code{vcov()}, normal. A robust
(sandwich) fit, from \code{cluster()}, \code{robust = TRUE}, or an \code{id} with
overlapping records, is multiplied by Stata's small-sample factor
M/(M - 1), M the number of clusters (every observation without
\code{cluster()}), as \verb{stcox, vce(cluster)}/\code{vce(robust)} report it;
likewise a robust \code{survival::survreg} fit (\verb{streg, vce(robust)}).
\code{vce = "model"} keeps survival's sandwich without the factor.
survival makes a fit robust by itself only for non-integer weights,
\code{cluster()} or \code{robust = TRUE}: integer (for instance rounded) weights
and \code{robust = FALSE} give the model-based variance, with a warning.
\strong{Ties:} \code{coxph()} defaults to Efron's approximation and \code{stcox} to
Breslow's, so on tied failure times the two differ (a one-time note
says so); fit with \code{ties = "breslow"} for Stata's numbers. \code{stcox}
refuses weights with \code{efron}, so a weighted Efron fit on tied failure
times has no Stata analogue: its log-likelihood, AIC and BIC are blank,
with a note. Without ties Efron's likelihood is Breslow's, so neither
note appears and the statistics are shown.
\item \code{survival::clogit} (Stata \code{clogit}): odds ratios, \code{vcov()}, normal;
the \code{strata()} term gives no row. Only \code{method = "exact"} (the default,
Stata's exact conditional likelihood, which survival fits unweighted);
\code{vce = "stata"} or \code{"model"}. Observations counts the rows; \code{events} and
\code{groups} add no row (Stata's clogit stores neither). As in Stata
tabtools 2.1.14, which classifies \code{clogit} like \code{logit} (odds ratios
whether or not it was fitted with \code{or}), the header is OR, \code{dimnonsig}
judges against 1, the methods sentence says "conditional logistic
regression", and a \code{logistic} model beside it loses its intercept row
under the automatic \code{nointercept}.
}

\code{vce = "model"} uses each fit's own \code{vcov()} and the distribution R's
\code{summary()} uses (t for \code{glm} families with an estimated dispersion).
A \code{glm} whose \code{converged} flag is \code{FALSE} is refused with
\code{tabtools_error_model_convergence}; refit before reporting its inference.
Explicit failed optimizer diagnostics from mixed, count, multinomial,
ordinal and GEE fits are refused with the same class. Mixed-model Hessian
failures use \code{tabtools_error_model_hessian}; unusable active \code{clm}
covariance uses \code{tabtools_error_model_covariance}. A singular random-effect
boundary or a gradient/conditioning advisory alone does not establish
failure when the estimated fixed-effect covariance remains usable.
Cox and \code{survreg} objects do not retain a complete convergence flag.
Right-censored Breslow/Efron Cox fits are refused when the stored iteration
count exceeds a literal or default iteration limit; mutable control objects
are not re-evaluated. Explicit limits must be literal control arguments
in the fitted call or in a qualified \code{survival::coxph.control()} call.
\code{survreg} fits whose likelihood is materially below
their nested intercept-only fit are refused. Other ambiguous survival
diagnostics remain the caller's responsibility to check at fit time.
An implicit-wave unstructured GEE working correlation that is singular
or indefinite is refused; explicit-wave or custom correlation designs
cannot be reconstructed from these stored diagnostics alone.
Robust or cluster-robust \code{lm} variances require more positive-weight
observations than the model rank. An ordinary robust \code{glm} variance
requires at least two positive-weight observations; these failures use
\code{tabtools_error_vce_sample_size}.

\code{ci_method = "profile"} replaces the Wald bounds with profile-likelihood
intervals (\code{confint()}), for \code{lm}, \code{glm} and \code{MASS::glm.nb} fits only,
and only with \code{vce = "stata"} or \code{"model"}: they come from the model's
likelihood, so beside a robust, clustered or design-based variance they
would not match the p-values, and they are refused (as are the other
classes, whose rows are always Wald; \code{\link[=tt_ci_methods]{tt_ci_methods()}} lists what a fit
supports). Weighted fits are profiled under the reading their Wald
interval uses: with \code{vce = "stata"}, a weighted binomial (0/1 response)
or Poisson \code{glm} is refitted with its weights rescaled to mean 1 (Stata's
\code{glm [aweight]} likelihood; an error if the refit does not reproduce the
fit), and \code{vce = "model"} profiles the fit as it is (\code{confint()}).
Non-integer, non-constant (inverse-probability) weights are profiled
too, with the warning that \code{vce = "robust"} is Stata's \verb{[pweight]}
reading (under \code{vce = "model"} as well, which profiles them as frequency
weights). The methods sentence then names the intervals "profile-likelihood" and
the p-values, which stay Wald, "from Wald tests". A profile that fails is an error, never a silent Wald
interval, and \code{stored$ci_method} records the method used. Stata's
intervals are Wald intervals.
Subclasses of \code{lm}/\code{glm} other than \code{survey::svyglm} and
\code{WeightIt::glm_weightit} (\code{mgcv::gam}, ...) carry their own variance and
degrees of freedom, which have not been verified against a Stata
command, and are refused. \code{vce = "robust"} and \code{"cluster"} are described
in the next section; \code{\link[=tt_vcov]{tt_vcov()}} returns the matrix behind any of them.
}

\section{Weights, robust variance, IPTW and marginal structural models}{

Stata reports robust standard errors whenever a model has probability
weights (\verb{logit y x [pw=w]}) or \code{vce(robust)}/\verb{vce(cluster id)}; R's
\code{glm(weights = w)} does not know which kind of weight \code{w} is. So \code{vce}
says it, per model:
\itemize{
\item \code{vce = "robust"}: \code{glm} (logit, probit, poisson, glm) HC0 times
N/(N - 1), z; \code{lm} (regress) HC1, t(N - k); \code{coxph} the dfbeta
sandwich (clusters from the fit's \code{cluster()}/\code{id}, else one per
row) times G/(G - 1), as \code{stcox} reports after \verb{stset ... [pw=w], id()}. This is Stata's \verb{[pweight]} (or \code{vce(robust)}) variance.
\item \code{vce = "cluster"} with \code{cluster = ~id} (a formula, a column name, or a
vector): \code{glm} and \code{coxph} times G/(G - 1), z; \code{lm} times
G/(G - 1) (N - 1)/(N - k), t(G - 1): Stata's \verb{vce(cluster id)}, the
variance of a pooled-logistic marginal structural model on
person-period data (\verb{logit y treat ... [pw=sw], vce(cluster id)}).
Giving \code{cluster} without \code{vce} means \code{vce = "cluster"}.
\item Either argument may be a list with one entry per model, so crude,
adjusted, IPTW and MSM columns can differ:
\code{regtab(crude, adj, iptw, msm, vce = list("stata", "stata", "robust", "cluster"), cluster = list(NULL, NULL, NULL, ~id))}. A single
\code{cluster} applies to the models whose \code{vce} is \code{"cluster"}.
\item \code{MASS::glm.nb} (nbreg) supports both: the sandwich over the
coefficients and ln(alpha) jointly, times N/(N - 1) (or G/(G - 1)), as
\code{nbreg [pweight]} reports it, the \code{lnalpha} row included.
\item Classes that support neither (\code{polr}, \code{clm}, \code{multinom}, \code{zeroinfl},
\code{hurdle}, mixed models, ...) refuse \code{"robust"}/\code{"cluster"} with an
error naming the class (\code{\link[=tt_vce_types]{tt_vce_types()}}); robust \code{survreg} fits get
Stata's G/(G - 1) under the default \code{vce = "stata"}.
\item \code{cluster} given while no model has \code{vce = "cluster"} is an error,
never ignored.
}

Weighted fits never get model-based standard errors silently, since
Stata's \verb{[pweight]} always reports robust ones:
\itemize{
\item Under the default \code{vce = "stata"}, \code{glm}/\code{lm} weights are analytic
weights (Stata \verb{[aweight]}, rescaled to mean 1), with model-based
standard errors; \code{glm.nb} weights are those of \code{nbreg [iweight]},
since \code{nbreg} takes no \verb{[aweight]}. When they are non-integer (any of
them) and non-constant, as inverse-probability weights are, regtab
warns that \code{vce = "robust"} is Stata's \verb{[pweight]} reading, but never
switches by itself. Integer
inverse-probability weights cannot be told from frequency weights and
are not flagged.
\item A weighted \code{geeglm} is \code{xtgee [pweight]}, which forces \code{vce(robust)}:
its default variance is the robust one.
\item A weighted \code{coxph} with survival's model-based variance (integer
weights, \code{robust = FALSE}) warns, as do weighted \code{polr}, \code{clm},
\code{multinom}, \code{zeroinfl}, \code{hurdle}, \code{survreg} and mixed-model fits with
non-integer weights: their weights act as frequency weights, and
regtab has no robust variance for them (refit \code{survreg} with
\code{robust = TRUE}).
}

Under probability weights (a robust or cluster \code{vce} on a weighted
\code{lm}/\code{glm}/\code{glm.nb}, a robust weighted \code{coxph}, \code{geeglm} with
\code{gee_as = "glm"}) Stata reports a pseudo-log-likelihood, and regtab
shows it with the AIC and BIC computed from it, as Stata's regtab does:
the weighted log-likelihood of \code{logit}, \code{probit}, \code{cloglog}, \code{poisson},
\code{nbreg} and \code{glm}'s Gamma and inverse Gaussian families (scale 1) and
Gaussian family (scale sum(w e^2)/N); \code{regress}'s \verb{[aweight]} log-likelihood (weights
normalised to mean 1); and \code{stcox}'s Breslow partial likelihood with the
weights normalised to mean 1. The pseudo R-squared of a logit or probit
fit is against the weighted constant-only model (\code{logit}/\code{probit}
e(r2_p); none with an offset, and none for other links, as \code{cloglog});
\code{poisson [pweight]} and Stata's \code{glm} command store none. A binomial
with trials, whose Stata \verb{[pweight]} log-likelihood has not been
checked, leaves the cells blank, with a note.
\code{svyglm} and \code{glm_weightit} report no likelihood (nor does Stata's
\verb{svy:} or \code{teffects}). Observations with a zero weight are not counted,
as Stata drops them.

\strong{AIC and BIC under few clusters.} After \code{vce(cluster)}, Stata's
e(rank) is the rank of the cluster-robust variance matrix, at most
G - 1. With fewer clusters than coefficients, Stata tabtools 2.1.12 then
counts the coefficients with a nonzero standard error, as
regtab counts the estimated parameters whatever the variance (2.1.11
used e(rank), \verb{logit y x1 x2 x3, vce(cluster c)} with 3 clusters giving
AIC -2 ll + 4).

\strong{Data changed after fitting.} A robust or cluster-robust variance is
computed from the rows the model was fitted on (see \code{\link[=tt_vcov]{tt_vcov()}}, "Rows
the variance is computed from"): if a \code{coxph}, \code{survreg} or
\code{model = FALSE} fit's data frame is re-sorted, filtered or edited after
fitting, a variance that needs those rows is an error (refit, or fit
with \code{model = TRUE}), never a silently different number. The table as a
whole follows the same rule: a fit that keeps no copy of its data
(\code{coxph}, \code{survreg}, \code{lm}/\code{glm.nb}/\code{polr} with \code{model = FALSE}, and
\code{nnet::multinom}, whose default is \code{model = FALSE}) is refused when its
data no longer reproduce it (removed, filtered or edited, or a fit read
from a file in a session without its data), since its labels, reference
rows, N and log-likelihood would come from other data than its
estimates; data re-sorted with their row names kept, or holding the same
observations in another order, are used. Storage options are either
irrelevant or refused by name: \code{lm(qr = FALSE)}, \code{ordinal::clm(model = FALSE)} and \code{pscl::zeroinfl}/\code{hurdle(model = FALSE)} are refused;
\code{glm(y = FALSE)}, \code{glm.nb(y = FALSE)}, \code{coxph(y = FALSE)}, \code{x = TRUE},
\verb{Hess =} and the others give the default fit's table. A fit read from a
file in a fresh session loads its class's package (\code{survival}, \code{nnet},
...) itself. A \code{coxph} fit with \code{tt()} terms, which survival does not
let keep its data, is checked on its observations, events and event
times, and gets the variances survival stores (\code{vce = "stata"} or
\code{"model"}, and the sandwich of a fit with \verb{cluster =} or
\code{robust = TRUE}); a variance that needs its rows is refused.
\itemize{
\item \code{survey::svyglm} (Stata \verb{svy:}): the design-based \code{vcov()}, t with the
design degrees of freedom \code{survey::degf()} (\verb{svyset _n [pw=w]}: N - 1;
\verb{svyset id [pw=w]}: clusters - 1), odds ratios for a
(quasi)binomial logit fit and IRRs for a (quasi)poisson log fit;
Observations is the unweighted number of rows, and a design from
\code{subset()} (Stata's \verb{svy, subpop()}) shows the subpopulation as
Subjects, as Stata's e(N_sub) (a design subset with \code{design[rows, ]}
is not recognised as a subpopulation and shows Observations). A
Gaussian identity fit shows the
weighted R-squared (\code{svy: regress} e(r2)). Use \code{glm()} with
\code{vce = "robust"} for \code{logit [pw]}'s z statistics. As in Stata
tabtools 2.1.11 and later, a \verb{svy:} fit is classified like its plain command:
\code{svy: logit} shows odds ratios with the intercept dropped, \code{svy: poisson} incidence rate ratios (tabtools 2.1.9 left \verb{svy:} fits
unclassified).
\item \code{WeightIt::glm_weightit}: its own M-estimation variance, which
accounts for the estimation of the weights (for an identity-link
\code{y ~ treat} fit, the ATE and standard error of Stata's
\verb{teffects ipw}); z statistics. \code{vce = "stata"} and \code{"model"} both
use it.
\item \code{geepack::geeglm} with \code{gee_as = "glm"} and an independence working
correlation: Stata's \verb{glm y x [pw=w], family() link() vce(cluster id)}
(odds ratios for a binomial logit fit, IRRs for a Poisson log fit, the
GEE sandwich times G/(G - 1)) instead of the default \code{xtgee} reading.
\item \code{vce_note = TRUE} (R only; off by default) appends a sentence naming
each model's non-default variance to the footnote, including the fits
that are robust without \code{vce = "robust"}: a weighted or clustered
\code{coxph}, a robust \code{survreg}, \code{crr}, a weighted \code{geeglm}.
\item A weighted \code{coxph} reports Stata's weighted subject count (\code{e(N_sub)}
after \verb{stset [pw=w], id()} is the sum of the subjects' weights, each
subject once) in the Subjects row, with a note saying it is a sum of
weights. The subjects are the fit's \code{id}, else its \code{cluster()} (or
\verb{cluster =}) variable. When the weights vary within a subject (which
\code{stset} refuses) the cell is blank, with a note. \code{coxph()} stops iterating when the relative change in
the log partial likelihood is below \code{eps} (\code{1e-9}), which on a flat
likelihood can leave a hazard ratio ~4e-9 from \code{stcox}'s; refit with
\code{control = coxph.control(eps = 1e-10)} when a last digit differs.
}

After a weighted fit, \code{marginaleffects} reproduces Stata's \code{margins} with
\code{vcov = tt_vcov(fit, "robust")} and \code{wts = w} (margins averages with the
estimation weights).

\strong{User-supplied variance (R only).} For an \code{lm}, \code{glm} or
\code{survival::coxph}/\code{clogit} fit, a \code{vce} entry may be a function
\verb{function(fit)} returning the covariance matrix of the coefficients, or
the matrix itself: \code{regtab(fit, vce = function(f) sandwich::vcovHC(f, "HC3"))}, or \code{vce = list("stata", V)} for two models. The matrix is
checked (square, symmetric, finite, named by the coefficients or in
their order; aliased coefficients may be left out) and used for every
standard error, interval, p-value and the \code{F} token; the estimates and
the other statistics are the fit's. The reference distribution is
\code{vce_df}: by default t with the residual degrees of freedom for \code{lm} and
the normal otherwise. A footnote says the standard errors are
user-supplied (every sink), whatever \code{vce_note}, and \code{stats = "vce"}
adds a row saying so for each model. None of Stata's variance
names is ever mapped to such a variance, and it cannot be combined with
\code{cluster}, \code{ci_method = "profile"}, a \code{\link[=tt_mi]{tt_mi()}} model or a data frame.

R's \code{glm()} stops iterating when the relative change in deviance is
below \code{epsilon} (default \code{1e-8}), which leaves non-canonical fits
(probit, cloglog, Gamma-log) up to ~1e-4 (relative) from the maximum
Stata reaches; refit with \code{control = glm.control(epsilon = 1e-12)} when
a cell differs from Stata in the last digit. Likewise, R does not drop
observations a covariate predicts perfectly, as Stata's \code{logit} does:
such coefficients (standard error above 100 on the link scale of a
binomial or Poisson model) raise a warning and print an enormous ratio
with a blank confidence interval. \code{glm(weights =)} are treated as
analytic (or binomial trials) weights: the \code{Observations} row counts rows
with a positive weight, not their sum as Stata's \verb{[fweight]} would, and
the standard errors, log-likelihood, AIC and BIC are those of Stata's
\code{glm [aweight]} (weights rescaled to mean 1; \code{vcov()} and \code{logLik()} of
a binomial or Poisson fit use them as they are, Stata's \verb{[iweight]}).
A binomial \code{cbind(successes, failures)} response, or a proportion
response with a value strictly between 0 and 1, has trials, not
analytic weights (Stata's \verb{glm, family(binomial m)}); a proportion
response with \verb{weights =} whose values are all 0 or 1 cannot be told
from 0/1 data with analytic weights, and is read as the latter. The
log-likelihood of a Gamma or inverse Gaussian \code{glm} is Stata's, at scale
1 (the estimated dispersion enters the standard errors only), where
\code{logLik()} plugs in deviance/n; quasi families have none.
Weighted \code{lm} fits report the log-likelihood, AIC and BIC of Stata's
\code{regress [aweight]} (weights rescaled to mean 1). The weights of
\code{MASS::polr}, \code{ordinal::clm}, \code{nnet::multinom}, \code{pscl::zeroinfl},
\code{pscl::hurdle} and \code{survival::survreg} fits act as case (frequency)
weights: their standard errors are those of the Stata command with
\verb{[fweight]}, and \code{Observations} (and BIC) use the sum of the weights, as
Stata's e(N) does (\code{survreg()} refuses zero weights: drop those rows with
\code{subset = w > 0}).
}

\section{Mixed models and GEE}{

\code{lme4::lmer()} fits correspond to Stata \code{mixed} (fit with \code{REML = FALSE}
for Stata's default ML), \code{lme4::glmer()} fits to \code{melogit},
\code{mepoisson}, \code{meprobit}, \code{mecloglog}, \code{menbreg} or \code{meglm} by family and
link, and \code{glmmTMB::glmmTMB()} fits likewise. Fixed effects are shown as
for fixed-effect models (odds ratios for a logit link, and so on).
Random-effects parameters follow every fixed-effect row, outermost
grouping level first, then the residual variance, with a rule above the
first: \verb{var(_cons)}, \code{var(age)}, \verb{cov(age,_cons)}, \code{var(e)} (a
bracketed grouping variable, \verb{var(_cons[school])}, with several levels
and for every \code{glmer} model), or with \code{relabel = TRUE} \verb{Variance: <group label> (Intercept)}, \code{Covariance: ...}, \verb{Residual Variance}, as
Stata words them (grouping levels that share a label are named by their
variables). For logit and cloglog \code{glmer} models the random intercept is
shown as the median odds (hazard) ratio, \verb{exp(sqrt(2 var) qnorm(0.75))}.
\code{noreeffects = TRUE} drops these rows. A negative binomial model
(\code{glmer.nb()}, or \code{glmmTMB(family = nbinom2)}) shows menbreg's
\code{lnalpha} (ln of 1/theta, no p-value) before the intercept.
Variance CIs are Wald intervals for the log standard deviation,
back-transformed; covariance CIs and p-values are Wald on the covariance
scale (delta method). The variance of these parameters, and for \code{glmer}
of the fixed effects too, is the inverse observed information from the
model's own deviance function (numerical second derivatives), as Stata
reports it; \code{lmer} fixed effects use \code{vcov()}, Stata \code{mixed}'s GLS
variance. \code{vce = "model"} changes only the fixed effects (lme4 reports no
variance for the random-effects parameters). A variance estimated at or
near zero has an ill-conditioned information matrix: its interval, and
through the joint inverse a neighbouring component's, can differ from
Stata's in the last digits (Stata's own are numerically fragile there);
an exact zero gets a blank interval, as in Stata.

\code{glmer} with \code{nAGQ} above 1 corresponds to Stata's default 7-point
adaptive quadrature (\code{nAGQ = 7}); its log-likelihood, AIC and BIC include
the saturated-model constant lme4's \code{logLik()} leaves out for \code{nAGQ > 1},
as Stata's e(ll) does. Models with several random effects are Laplace
fits in \code{glmer} (Stata \code{intmethod(laplace)}). For probit and cloglog
links and for \code{glmer.nb()}, lme4's Laplace approximation is not Stata's,
so estimates and standard errors can differ in the last digit (regtab
says so once per session); \code{glmmTMB::glmmTMB()} reproduces Stata's
Laplace fits exactly and is the fit to use for exact parity. \code{nAGQ = 0}
fits are refused. \code{glmmTMB} fits with a \code{ziformula} or \code{dispformula}
submodel are refused (Stata's me* commands have no such equations), as
is the \code{nbinom1} family (menbreg's constant dispersion, not checked
against Stata).

\code{mecloglog} fixed effects are shown as hazard ratios, \code{exp()} of the
coefficients, as Stata tabtools 2.1.10 and later show them (2.1.9 printed
the raw log-hazard coefficients under its HR header).

Stats: \code{groups} counts the innermost level's groups; \code{icc} is the sum of
the random-intercept variances over itself plus the residual variance
(the latent \code{pi^2/3}, 1, or \code{pi^2/6} for logit, probit, cloglog), and is
not computed for count families. A factor keeps its header row in models
with several grouping levels, as in Stata tabtools 2.1.11 and later (2.1.9
lost it).

\code{nlme::lme()} fits also correspond to Stata \code{mixed} (\code{method = "ML"} for
Stata's default; nlme's own default, REML, is \verb{mixed, reml}), with the
same rows, labels, intervals and statistics as an \code{lmer} fit of the same
model. Fixed-effect standard errors are the fit's \code{varFix}, the GLS
variance Stata reports, with z statistics; \code{summary.lme()} shows other
ones for an ML fit (it rescales them by \code{sqrt(N / (N - p))} and uses t
distributions), and regtab never reads it. An unstructured random-effects
covariance (\code{pdSymm}, \code{pdLogChol}, the default) is Stata's
\code{cov(unstructured)}, \code{pdDiag} its \code{cov(independent)} (a variance row per
component and no covariance row), and nested levels (\code{~ 1 | zone/location})
\verb{|| zone: || location:}. lme never estimates a variance of exactly 0 or a
correlation of +/-1, so regtab maximises the fit's likelihood again with
the variances bounded at 0, as lme4 does: a term whose maximum is on
that boundary is shown at it (exact zeros) with blank intervals, as for
lmer, and a fit that stopped short of its maximum (typically on the flat
ridge towards such a boundary) is refused with a hint to refit. Unused factor
levels are dropped, and a fit without \verb{data =} is read from its
formula's environment, as lme does. Refused, with an error naming the feature:
\verb{correlation =} and \verb{weights =} structures (Stata's \verb{mixed, residuals()}
parameters have no rows in regtab's layout), a fixed residual standard
deviation (\code{lmeControl(sigma = )}), \code{pdIdent}, \code{pdCompSymm} and
\code{pdBlocked} covariances of several random effects, and a fit whose data
no longer reproduce it (fitted with \code{keep.data = FALSE} and the data
changed or gone since). \code{nlme::gls()} and \code{nlme::nlme()} fits are not
supported.

\code{geepack::geeglm()} fits correspond to Stata \code{xtgee}, which Stata
tabtools 2.1.12 shows by glm's family/link rule: odds ratios for a
binomial logit and incidence rate ratios for a Poisson log link
(exponentiated, the intercept dropped automatically), coefficients with
the intercept otherwise (2.1.11 showed every xtgee on the
linear-predictor scale); xtgee's default model-based variance (the scale fixed at 1 for binomial
and Poisson families). A fit with \code{scale.fix = TRUE} is \verb{xtgee, scale(#)}: \code{scale.value} (which \code{geeglm()} takes as one value per
observation) must be one positive constant, e.g. \code{rep(2, nrow(d))} with
\code{data = d}, and a varying one is refused. geepack stores only the
\code{scale.value} expression, not its value, so the expression may refer only
to the fit's data (its columns, or the data argument's name): a scale
held in another variable (\code{scale.value = sc}, \code{rep(2, n)}) is refused,
since its value at fit time cannot be established (it may have changed
since). With a working correlation other than
independence, geepack's \code{scale.fix = TRUE} fit estimates the
correlation with the dispersion held at its start value, while xtgee's
correlation estimate does not depend on the scale, so the estimates
differ from \verb{xtgee, scale(#)}'s (a note says so; about 1e-4 relative on
the package's fixture). geepack takes each run of equal \code{id}
values as a cluster, so a fit on data not sorted by \code{id} (more clusters
than ids) is refused: Stata's \code{xtgee} groups by the panel variable.
\code{vce = "model"} is the fit's own \code{vcov()}, which follows geepack's
\code{std.err}: the sandwich for the default \code{"san.se"} (Stata's
\code{vce(robust)} without its \code{G/(G - 1)} factor), geepack's jackknife
variance for \code{"jack"}, \code{"j1s"} or \code{"fij"}. \code{vce = "robust"},
\code{"cluster"}, the default variance of a weighted fit and \code{gee_as = "glm"} always use the stored GEE sandwich (\code{fit$geese$vbeta}) times
\code{G/(G - 1)}, whatever \code{std.err} the fit chose. There is
no AIC or BIC; \code{stats = "qic"} (or \code{"aic"}, then labelled \code{QICu}) shows
QICu = deviance + 2k when the scale is fixed at 1, else a note. Its
additive constant follows Stata's deviance; compare QICu only between
models on the same data. geepack's default convergence tolerance
(\code{geese.control(epsilon = 1e-4)}) leaves estimates about 1e-6 from
xtgee's; \code{epsilon = 1e-6} reproduces xtgee's default fit.
}

\section{Multiple imputation}{

A multiply imputed model, a \code{mice} \code{mira} (\code{with(imp, glm(...))}) or
\code{\link[=tt_mi]{tt_mi()}} of a list of fits, is shown as one model, as Stata shows
\verb{collect: mi estimate: <command>}: rows and labels from the first
imputation, numbers pooled by Rubin's rules over each imputation's
\code{tt_vcov(fit, vce, cluster)}, with the degrees of freedom of Stata's
\verb{mi estimate} (Barnard and Rubin's small-sample degrees of freedom for
\code{lm}, whose completed-data degrees of freedom are finite, and Rubin's
large-sample degrees of freedom for \code{glm} and Cox models, except a \code{glm}
with an estimated dispersion under \code{vce = "model"}, whose t reference
gives it the small-sample ones; see \code{\link[=tt_mi]{tt_mi()}}). \code{stats = c("mi_m", "fmi")} show the number of imputations
and the largest fraction of missing information; the log-likelihood,
AIC, BIC, R-squared, Root MSE and F are blank, as \verb{mi estimate} reports
none, and Observations (Subjects, Events) show when every imputation
has the same. The methods sentence adds "with multiple imputation".
\code{lm}, \code{glm}, \code{coxph} (Fine-Gray included) and \code{cmprsk::crr} fits are
pooled; other classes are refused. A pooled \code{mice} result (\code{mipo}) is
refused: pass the \code{mira}. With \code{vce = "cluster"}, \code{cluster} is read
from each imputation (a variable: from its data, or for a \code{mira} from
the completed dataset \code{with()} evaluated it in) or used for every
imputation (a vector). Rows and labels come from the first imputation:
\code{mice}'s completed data carry no variable labels, so a \code{mira} table
shows variable names unless the data were labelled (see \code{\link[=tt_mi]{tt_mi()}}).
Use \code{tt_mi(fits, observation_ids = ids, sample_check = "strict")} when
row names no longer retain observation identity. The optional
\code{meta$mi_sample_identity} list has one element per model (\code{NULL} for
non-MI models), recording the method (\code{"explicit_ids"}, \code{"row_names"}
or \code{"counts_only"}), per-imputation observation counts and strict policy.
It contains no observation IDs; row-name checks still depend on stable
original identifiers and counts alone do not establish identity.

The effect scale follows the model class, as for a single fit. Stata's
\verb{mi estimate} reports coefficients unless \verb{mi estimate} itself is given
an eform option (\verb{mi estimate, or: logit ...}), and Stata tabtools
2.1.12 reads that rule from the command line, so that \verb{mi estimate: logit} and \verb{mi estimate, or: logit} both show odds ratios; R has no
such prefix, and the tables agree. The one case R cannot express is an
eform option given to \verb{mi estimate} for a family Stata shows as
coefficients (\verb{mi estimate, eform: glm ..., family(gaussian)}: \code{exp(b)}
in Stata; coefficients in R).
}

\section{Session destinations}{

An explicitly supplied non-NULL \code{sheet} enables inherited workbook and
Markdown destinations from \code{\link[=tabtools_options]{tabtools_options()}}. Default or NULL sheet does
not request inherited output. Explicit paths still export, and explicit NULL
destinations opt out. A requested sheet without a workbook gives
\code{tabtools_warning_sheet_without_workbook} before output; \code{options(warn = 2)}
interrupts the call. See \code{\link[=tabtools_options]{tabtools_options()}} for append/history behavior.
}

\section{Data-frame input}{

A model regtab does not support can be passed as a data frame shaped
like \code{\link[broom.helpers:tidy_plus_plus]{broom.helpers::tidy_plus_plus()}} output, one row per coefficient:
\itemize{
\item required: \code{term} (character, no missing or repeated values within an
equation) and \code{estimate} (numeric), the estimate already on the
display scale (use \code{exponentiate = TRUE} for ratios; nothing is
transformed). A missing \code{estimate} is an omitted coefficient;
\item optional statistics, all numeric and never \code{NaN}: \code{std.error}
(positive and finite), \code{conf.low} and \code{conf.high} (both or neither,
\code{conf.low <= conf.high}), \code{p.value} (in [0, 1]), and \code{df} or
\code{df.error} (positive, \code{Inf} for the normal distribution). An infinite
estimate or bound (\code{exp()} of a huge coefficient) prints blank, as for
a fitted model. Factors and character columns are refused rather than
converted: \code{as.numeric()} of a factor gives its level codes.
Supplied intervals and p-values are shown as they are. A row with
neither bound gets a Wald interval from \code{std.error}, and, when the
data frame has no \code{p.value} column at all, a Wald p-value (a supplied
\code{p.value} column is kept as it is, missing entries included). These
use Student's t with the row's \code{df} (or \code{df.error}) when given, else
the normal distribution;
\item the scale of \code{std.error}: on a ratio scale (OR, HR, IRR, RRR, SHR, TR)
a derived interval or p-value needs \code{attr(x, "se_scale")}: \code{"link"}
when \code{std.error} is the standard error of log(\code{estimate}), as
\code{\link[broom.helpers:tidy_plus_plus]{broom.helpers::tidy_plus_plus()}} with \code{exponentiate = TRUE} returns it
(z = log(estimate) / std.error, interval exp(log(estimate) +/- q
std.error)), or \code{"estimate"} when it is on the ratio's own scale (z =
(estimate - 1) / std.error, interval estimate +/- q std.error).
Without it such rows are refused, by name, rather than guessed. On
the \code{Coef.} scale (and for ancillary rows) \code{std.error} is on the scale
of \code{estimate}. Ratio estimates must be positive;
\item the effect scale: \code{attr(x, "effect_scale")} (the header; a ratio
label, \code{OR}, \code{HR}, \code{RR}, \code{IRR}, \code{RRR}, \code{SHR}, \code{TR}, \code{PR}, \code{GMR},
\code{exp(b)} or its spelled-out name, has null 1, and a difference label,
\code{Coef.}, \code{Beta}, \code{RD}, \code{MD}, ..., null 0; any other label needs
\code{attr(x, "null_value")}, 0 or 1, whenever the null is used by a
derived statistic or \code{dimnonsig}), else \code{attr(x, "stata_cmd")}, else
broom.helpers' own record that it exponentiated the estimates
(\code{attr(x, "exponentiate")}, with its \code{coefficients_label}, e.g. \code{OR},
as the header), else \code{Coef.}. A broom \code{statistic} column that shows
the estimates were exponentiated (log(estimate) / std.error, as
\code{broom::tidy(exponentiate = TRUE)} returns it) on a frame taken as
coefficients is refused, and so is a declared \code{se_scale = "link"} it
contradicts, or any \code{se_scale} on a frame without ratio rows;
\item the confidence level of supplied intervals: \code{attr(x, "conf.level")}
(which \code{\link[broom.helpers:tidy_plus_plus]{broom.helpers::tidy_plus_plus()}} sets) or a constant
\code{conf.level} column (a proportion or a percentage), else 95\%. With
\code{level} left at its default, regtab uses that level (the data frames
must agree); an explicit \code{level} that differs is refused: regtab
cannot recompute supplied intervals. \code{vce} must be \code{"stata"} for a
data frame, which carries its own standard errors;
\item other tools: \code{parameters::model_parameters(exponentiate = TRUE)}
reports the standard error of the ratio itself (delta method), so it
is \code{se_scale = "estimate"}, unlike broom (\code{"link"}); rename its
columns first (\code{Parameter} to \code{term}, \code{Coefficient} to \code{estimate},
\code{SE} to \code{std.error}, \code{CI_low}/\code{CI_high} to \code{conf.low}/\code{conf.high},
\code{p} to \code{p.value});
\item row roles, from the frame's structure and never from a label (a
covariate named \code{p}, \code{alpha} or \code{lnsigma} stays a covariate): a \code{role}
column (\code{"coef"}, \code{"intercept"}, \code{"cutpoint"}, \code{"ancillary"}); else
Stata's \code{/}, \verb{_diparm#}, \code{lnsigma}, \code{lnalpha}, \code{ln_p} and \code{lngamma}
equations, and a term written in the \code{/} equation (\verb{/sigma}), hold
ancillary parameters (\verb{/cut#} a cutpoint); else \code{var_type}
\code{"cutpoint"}/\code{"ancillary"}, and a \code{var_type = "intercept"} row other
than \code{(Intercept)}/\verb{_cons} (broom.helpers types a model's thresholds
and \code{Log(scale)} as intercepts) is a cutpoint when broom's \code{coef.type}
says so or the term is a threshold (\code{a|b}), else ancillary.
Cutpoints and ancillary rows keep the estimate's own scale (null 0),
follow \code{nointercept}/\code{keepintercept} with the intercept, and are keyed
in Stata's \code{/} equation (\verb{/::sigma}), so they join a fitted model's
ancillary rows of the same name;
\item optional description columns: \code{label}, \code{var_label}, \code{variable},
\code{var_type} (\code{"intercept"}, \code{"continuous"}, \code{"categorical"},
\code{"dichotomous"}, \code{"interaction"}, \code{"cutpoint"}, \code{"ancillary"}),
\code{role}, \code{reference_row}, \code{header_row}
(header rows are dropped; regtab writes its own), \code{contrasts_type}
(only treatment contrasts: a \code{"sum"} or \code{"helmert"} factor has no
reference level), and \code{key};
\item keys: a factor level is keyed like a fitted model's level, \verb{2.treat},
so \code{regtab(fit, tidy_plus_plus(fit))} shares the level rows of \code{treat}
(and \code{keep = "2.treat"} works). The two columns of that example can
show different intervals: broom.helpers' default intervals for a \code{glm}
are profile-likelihood ones, while regtab computes Wald intervals, and
supplied intervals are shown as they are. The code is the level's label when it
is an integer, else its position among the variable's rows (reference
row included), as for a fitted factor without Stata value codes. A
variable without a reference row keeps its \code{term} as the key, because
its base level's position is unknown. A \code{key} column (Stata keys such
as \verb{2.treat}, \code{age}, \verb{_cons}) overrides the derived key for the rows
where it is not missing. An interaction row is keyed like a fitted
model's (\verb{2.g#c.age}) when each part of its term is a level row or a
continuous variable of the data frame, else by its term;
\item an equation column, \code{equation} (or \code{y.level}, or \code{component}), turns
on Stata's multi-equation layout: rows read \verb{<equation>: <label>},
with Stata's equation names translated as regtab does (\code{inflate} is
"Inflation equation", \code{selection}/\code{selection_ll}/\code{selection_ul}
"Selection equation", \code{lnsigma} "Scale", \code{/} and \verb{_diparm*}
"Ancillary") and underscores shown as spaces.
}

Attributes describe the model: \code{"stata_cmd"} (the Stata command word,
which sets the estimate header, the null value for \code{dimnonsig}, and the
automatic \code{nointercept} as for a collected Stata model, e.g. \code{"logit"},
\code{"churdle"}); \code{"effect_scale"}, \code{"null_value"}, \code{"se_scale"} and
\code{"conf.level"} (above); \code{"glance"}, a
named list or one-row data frame with numeric scalar entries (\code{NA} or
\code{NULL} for unavailable statistics), with any of \code{nobs}, \code{n_sub}, \code{logLik}, \code{df}
(Stata's e(rank)), \code{AIC}, \code{BIC} (recomputed from \code{logLik} and \code{df} when
both are given), \code{r.squared}, \code{adj.r.squared}, \code{pseudo.r.squared},
\code{groups}, \code{icc}, \code{qic}, \code{nevent} (Events), \code{rmse} (Root MSE), \code{F} (the F
statistic, shown only when \code{"stata_cmd"} is \code{"regress"}, \code{"anova"},
\code{"areg"}, \code{"xtreg"}, \code{"ivregress"} or \code{"cnsreg"}, the linear models Stata
shows it for), \code{nimp} (Imputations) and \code{fmi} (Largest FMI), for
\code{stats}; and \code{"model_id"},
\code{"outcome_id"} for \code{\link[=as_forest_data]{as_forest_data()}}.
}

\section{Ordinal, multinomial, count, and survival models}{

Each class is shown as the Stata command it estimates, with that
command's default standard errors (\code{vce = "model"} uses the fit's own
\code{vcov()} instead); every test and interval is normal (z):
\itemize{
\item \code{MASS::polr}, \code{ordinal::clm} (Stata \code{ologit}, OR; \code{oprobit} and other
links, Coef.): the covariates, then one row per cutpoint, \code{cut1},
\code{cut2}, ..., on the raw scale with no p-value, dropped by the automatic
\code{nointercept} of ologit and relabelled by \code{cutlabels}. Standard errors:
the analytic observed information (\code{clm}'s own; for \code{polr}, computed at
its estimates instead of \code{optim()}'s numerical Hessian). \code{clm} fits
need flexible thresholds and no nominal or scale effects. With an
\code{offset()}, the Pseudo R-squared is blank, as Stata's \code{ologit}/
\code{oprobit} report no e(ll_0); so is a \code{logit}/\code{probit} glm's (Stata
stores no e(r2_p)), while a Poisson glm's is against the constant-only
model with the offset, as Stata's \code{poisson}. Only logit, probit and
log-link Poisson glm fits have one at all: Stata's \code{cloglog} and \code{glm}
commands store no e(r2_p).
\item \code{nnet::multinom} (Stata \code{mlogit}, RRR): one equation per non-base
outcome, rows \verb{<outcome label>: <label>}; a factor reads as in the
single-equation layout inside each equation, \verb{Secondary: Smoking status} above \code{Secondary:   Former} (Stata tabtools 2.1.12; 2.1.11
showed \verb{Secondary: 1.smoking}). As in Stata, factor levels (and interaction
cells with a factor) come first in each equation only when the base
outcome has the lowest value code; otherwise the rows follow the
formula. The base outcome is the response's first level, where
Stata's \code{mlogit} without \code{baseoutcome()} takes the most frequent
outcome: the table matches \verb{mlogit, baseoutcome(#)} with that first
level, and \code{\link[stats:relevel]{stats::relevel()}} the response to match another; when the
first level is not the most frequent outcome, \code{regtab()} says so once
per session. Its code is
read from the \code{"labels"} attribute (\code{\link[=tt_as_factor]{tt_as_factor()}}) or integer level
texts, and taken as the lowest when neither exists. \code{relevel()} drops
the attribute: restore it (\code{attr(y, "labels") <- codes}) to reproduce
\verb{mlogit, baseoutcome()} with a base other than the lowest value.
Weights enter the information matrix; a count-matrix response
(\code{cbind(n1, n2, n3) ~ x}) gives the same table as the one-row-per-unit
fit. An \code{offset()} term (an n x outcomes matrix, or a vector for a
binary outcome) enters the information and the constant-only model of
the pseudo R-squared; Stata's \code{mlogit} has no offset, so this is an R
extension. \code{multinom()} keeps no copy of its data unless
\code{model = TRUE}: the data are read again and used only when they
reproduce the fit (rows, response and fitted probabilities), else the
fit is refused. Weight decay (\code{decay > 0}, a penalised fit with no
Stata equivalent) is refused. \code{multinom()} stops ~1e-4 (relative) from
the maximum; refit with \verb{reltol = 1e-12, maxit = 1000} when a last
digit differs.
\item \code{pscl::zeroinfl} (Stata \code{zip}/\code{zinb}) and \code{pscl::hurdle} (Stata's
\code{churdle} layout), Coef.: rows \verb{<dependent variable label>: <label>},
\verb{Inflation equation: <label>} (zeroinfl) or \verb{Selection equation: <label>} (hurdle), and, for negative binomial counts, \code{Ancillary: lnalpha} (\code{-log(theta)}) and \code{Ancillary: alpha}. Standard errors: the
observed information from the analytic score (pscl's \code{vcov()} comes
from a numerical Hessian at estimates \code{optim()} stops ~1e-4 from the
maximum).
\item \code{MASS::glm.nb} (Stata \code{nbreg}): with \code{keepintercept}, the rows
\code{lnalpha} (ln of 1/theta, from the joint information) and \code{alpha}
precede the intercept, without a p-value.
\item \code{survival::survreg} with a Weibull, exponential, lognormal or
loglogistic distribution (Stata \verb{streg, time}): time ratios (TR),
\code{exp()} of the log-time coefficients with \code{exp()}-transformed
confidence bounds, as Stata tabtools 2.1.10 and later show them (2.1.9
printed the coefficients unexponentiated). With \code{keepintercept}, the
ancillary rows \code{ln_p}, \code{p}, \code{1/p} (Weibull), \code{lnsigma}, \code{sigma}
(lognormal) or \code{lngamma}, \code{gamma} (loglogistic) precede the intercept,
on their own scale, and the intercept is shown as \code{exp()} of the
constant, as in Stata tabtools 2.1.12 (2.1.10 and 2.1.11 also
exponentiated the lognormal and loglogistic ancillary rows, which
regtab never copied). A covariate named like one of these parameters
(\code{ln_p}, \code{lnsigma}, ...) is shown in its own row beside it, with the
same label, as Stata 2.1.12 shows it. The log-likelihood is
streg's, which leaves out the Jacobian of the log-time transform. A
Gaussian \code{survreg} is Stata \code{intreg} (Coef., intercept kept, a \code{sigma}
row before it); an \code{AER::tobit()} fit is Stata \code{tobit} (the residual
variance \verb{var(e.<y>)} after the intercept, like a random-effects row).
Other distributions (logistic, t, extreme value, rayleigh) have no
Stata equivalent and are refused.
\item Fine-Gray (Stata \code{stcrreg}, SHR): \code{survival::coxph()} on
\code{survival::finegray()} data, with standard errors from the sandwich
clustered by subject times N/(N - 1), Stata's \code{vce(robust)};
\code{coxph()}'s own variance treats every expanded record as independent.
A fit is Fine-Gray when it is weighted, its response is
\code{Surv(start, stop, status)}, and its data carry \code{finegray()}'s mark
(its \code{"event"} attribute), whatever the columns are called; an
unweighted fit on such data is a Cox model; \code{regtab(finegray = TRUE/FALSE)} declares it. The subjects are the fit's \code{cluster()}/\code{id}
(\code{coxph(..., cluster = id)} or \code{coxph(..., id = id)}, with the id kept
in the \code{finegray()} formula) when given. Without one, they are read
only from \code{finegray()} output of right-censored data (each subject's
records together, the first with weight 1 at the time origin, the
later ones with censoring weights below 1), in \code{finegray()}'s own row
order, which data re-sorted with \code{[} keep in their row names. Delayed
entry and counting-process input (\code{finegray(Surv(start, stop, event) ~ ., id = id)}) can write several weight-1 records per subject, so
there, and for data re-sorted with the row names reset, case weights,
or data without \code{finegray()}'s mark, the variance is an error until the
id is on the fit.
Labels \code{finegray()} drops are restored from the data it was given when
that data frame is found in the model's environment. The weighted Cox
fit differs from Stata's by ~1e-3 in the estimates and ~1e-4 in the
standard errors; \code{cmprsk::crr()} reproduces \code{stcrreg} exactly (its
variance times N/(N - 1)), with rows labelled by the covariate
matrix's column names: name them, \code{cov1 = cbind(treated = d$treated)}
(an unnamed \code{cov1 = d$treated} shows \code{co$treated1}).
}

Beside a multi-equation model (\code{multinom}, \code{zeroinfl}, \code{hurdle}), and
without a mixed model, every model takes Stata's \code{coleq#colname} layout:
a single-equation model's rows read \verb{<dependent variable label>: <label>} (a factor as a header row above its indented levels, with a
Reference row, as in Stata tabtools 2.1.12; its intercept \code{Intercept}),
so a Poisson or negative binomial model of the
same outcome shares the \code{zip}/\code{zinb} count equation's rows; ancillary
rows read \verb{Ancillary: <name>} (\code{Ancillary: cut1}, \code{Ancillary: lnalpha};
\code{cutlabels} replace the whole label, as in Stata 2.1.12);
survival models' equation is Stata's \verb{_t}, "Analysis time when record
ends"; intreg's is \code{model}, with its \code{Scale: Intercept} row. Every
model's equations are kept, with blank cells where a model lacks one, as
in Stata tabtools 2.1.11 and later (2.1.9 kept only the equations of its
first model).
}

\examples{
fit1 <- glm(breaks ~ wool, family = poisson, data = warpbreaks)
fit2 <- glm(breaks ~ wool + tension, family = poisson, data = warpbreaks)
regtab(fit1, fit2, models = c("Crude", "Adjusted"), stats = c("n", "aic"))

# A linear model with an interaction, written to an Excel sheet: the
# table is then returned invisibly, so assign it and print it
xlsx <- tempfile(fileext = ".xlsx")
tab <- regtab(lm(breaks ~ wool * tension, data = warpbreaks), stars = TRUE,
              xlsx = xlsx, sheet = "Linear")
tab
# A methods sentence for the paper
tab$stored$methods
}
